how that  informs your sense of government or or  your limits to government why government  maybe shouldn't be calling most of the  shots at anybody's life I keep my  personal views or try to on religion  separate from my public life here in  Congress it's important to me my faith  is important to me I'll be home this  Sunday with my family in church and I  think it's it's helpful in guiding my  personal life but I don't want to impose  those views on anybody else here so in a  way that's also I mean  kind of Laboratory of you know a  laboratory of democracy of experiment  understanding that things that are  personal and central to people's value  systems should be even people should be  able to experiment as much as they want  as long as they're not forcing other  people into certain behaviors I think we  should respect other people's religions  other people's views of the world and  try not to impose our own on them I mean  and this is this is what government is  about government is not about protecting  us from ourselves  government fundamentally is about  providing some of these services you  can't provide in the private sector but  also just protecting people from  infringing on other people's rights or  views just a final comment you've been  you know you've been in office now for a  few months are you optimistic that your  children will inherit a you know in  America that is more free and more  prosperous than the one that you  inherited as you know or when you showed  up here I mean are you fearful of the  future are you optimistic that we will  we will do well I am I am an optimist at  heart so I am optimistic that our  country is going to go forward but I  don't think it's going to be because of  anything we do here in Congress I think  that private industry and private  individuals will succeed in spite of  Congress's inability to get out of their  way sometimes but I'm also an optimist  that we can make a change here that we  can have a difference that's why I came  here and I'm ready to stick it out and  fight the fight  well we'll leave it at that thank you  very much representative Thomas Massie  of the Kentucky's fourth congressional  district thank you very much thank you  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

